As usual, I'm late to the party (time zones etc). But thanks, all, for the rich 
discussion sparked by my stupid question (though really, it's a shame that the 
"Doraemon" comic hasn't been translated into English). I look forward to trying 
out Henrik Singmann's afex package and reading the refs written and/or suggested 
by Roger Levy.

I just want to reiterate that whatever the experts eventually settle on (it 
always amuses me, as a former math BA, how unscientific statistics is), you 
GOTTA be able to test x1, x2, and x1:x2 - all of them - in a y ~ x1*x2 model. So 
much in psycholinguistics depends on it, like the additive method of Sternberg 
for testing the independence of cognitive processes.

At the same time, it must also be legit, in principle, to test interactions 
without testing main effects, since that's basically what the two-way 
chi-squared test does:

mat = matrix(c(9,1,1,9), ncol = 2)
chisq.test(mat)$p.value

y=c(9,1,1,9); x1=c(-1,-1,1,1); x2=c(-1,1,-1,1)
summary(glm(y~x1*x2,family=poisson))$coefficients["x1:x2","Pr(>|z|)"]/2

- jm

