Just a follow-up on this.  First, if you sum-code both x1 and x2, then for those of (x1,x2) that are dichotomous you will be able to interpret their parameter estimate and associated significance levels in the y ~ x1*x2 models in the way that you want — as the “main effect” in the presence of the interaction.  (I think you know this already but it bears reiterating.)

If one or both of (x1,x2) has three or more levels and you want to do an omnibus test on its main effect in the presence of the interaction — that’s exactly what my writeup is about.

> At the same time, it must also be legit, in principle, to test interactions without testing main effects, since that's basically what the two-way chi-squared test does:
> 
> mat = matrix(c(9,1,1,9), ncol = 2)
> chisq.test(mat)$p.value

Wait: this chi-squared test is testing the interaction, not the main effects.

> 
> y=c(9,1,1,9); x1=c(-1,-1,1,1); x2=c(-1,1,-1,1)
> summary(glm(y~x1*x2,family=poisson))$coefficients["x1:x2","Pr(>|z|)"]/2

This model *is* "testing the main effects in the presence of an interaction”.  However — and this is very important!!! — it is not at all clear what meanings these "main effects” have.  Whatever they are, they are *not* the same thing as the main effects in a y~x1+x2 model.  Here’s an example of this:


> dat <- data.frame(y=c(rep(0,10),rep(1,90),rep(0,90),rep(1,10),rep(0,40),rep(1,60),rep(0,50),rep(1,50)), x1=rep(c(-1,-1,1,1),each=100), x2=rep(c(-1,1,-1,1),each=100))
> summary(glm(y~x1*x2,data=dat,family="binomial"))
[…]
Coefficients:
            Estimate Std. Error z value Pr(>|z|)    
(Intercept)   0.1014     0.1378   0.736    0.462    
x1            0.1014     0.1378   0.736    0.462    
x2           -1.2000     0.1378  -8.708  < 2e-16 ***
x1:x2         0.9972     0.1378   7.237  4.6e-13 ***
[…]
> summary(glm(y~x1+x2,data=dat,family="binomial"))
[…]Coefficients:
            Estimate Std. Error z value Pr(>|z|)    
(Intercept)   0.1261     0.1125   1.120    0.263    
x1            0.1261     0.1125   1.120    0.263    
x2           -0.9765     0.1127  -8.665   <2e-16 ***
[…]

Note that the x1 and x2 main effects (and the intercept too!) have different parameter estimates in the presence of the interaction than in the absence of the interaction.  This follows from the nonlinearity in the relationship between the linking function and the mean of the response variable.  It is only in the special case of linear models that you can even talk about the main effects (and indeed any interactions of terms lower than the highest-order interaction containing those terms) as potentially having the same meaning in models with and without a higher-order interaction.

Roger

