>> y=c(9,1,1,9); x1=c(-1,-1,1,1); x2=c(-1,1,-1,1)
>> summary(glm(y~x1*x2,family=poisson))$coefficients["x1:x2","Pr(>|z|)"]/2

BTW I shouldn't have divided by 2 here - I clicked SEND too early. The two 
interaction tests are actually related as shown in the plot generated by the R 
code below - I assume the noisiness comes from the "main effects" (which 
actually aren't) in the Poisson regression:

B = 1000
chi.X2 = numeric(B)
pois.z = numeric(B)
for (i in 1:B) {
  # Fake data
  obs = sample(50,4)
  # chi-squared test statistic for interaction
  mat = matrix(obs, ncol = 2)
  chi2 = chisq.test(mat)
  chi.X2[i] = chi2$statistic
  # Poisson regression (Wald) statistic for interaction
  y=obs; x1=c(-1,-1,1,1); x2=c(-1,1,-1,1)
  pois = summary(glm(y~x1*x2,family=poisson))
  pois.z[i] = pois$coefficients["x1:x2","z value"]
}
plot(pois.z,chi.X2)

You can see why I left mathematics for linguistics - I'm bad at reasoning things 
